Ayers Rock Helicopters, piloted by Ian Wallace and Michael Van Der Zypp. Technical advice was provided by David West and Julian Bickersteth of International Conservation Services, Sydney. The permission of the Anangu people, the traditional owners of Uluru, to tell this story is gratefully acknowledged.\n\n## Disclosure statement\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNithyani Anandakugan - Harvard International Review (https://hir.harvard.edu/author/nithyani/)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * # Nithyani Anandakugan ... ## The Sri Lankan Civil War and Its History, Revisited in 2020 ... ## “The Uluru Statement from the Heart\": Contextualizing a First Nations Declaration\n  * # Nithyani Anandakugan\n\n  * 5 posts\n\nArts & Culture\n\n## From Defending Islands to Defending the Soccer Pitch: History of the 1986 England-Argentina World Cup\n\n02.May.2022\n\nNithyani Anandakugan\n\nAsia\n\n## The Sri Lankan Civil War and Its History, Revisited in 2020\n\n31.Aug.2020\n\nNithyani Anandakugan\n\nThe Americas\n\n## Hopes For A Rainy Day: A History of Bolivia's Water Crisis\n\n02.Jul.2020\n\nNithyani Anandakugan\n\nOceania\n\n## “The Uluru Statement from the Heart\": Contextualizing a First Nations Declaration\n\n06.Jan.2020\n\nNithyani Anandakugan\n\nEnergy & Environment\n\n## Swimming Against A Current: Cambodia's Struggle with the Mekong River\n\n20.Oct.2019\n\nNithyani Anandakugan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReturning Uluru (https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/returning-uluru-david-west)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: last week; This was done in order to return Uluru to a condition as close as possible to that which had prevailed for thousands of years. ... This work was described in a paper presented at the International Institute for Conservation (IIC) 2020 Edinburgh Congress by my colleague at International Conservation Services, Julian Bickersteth. ... It is an honour to reflect on my small contribution to the process of returning Uluru to the traditional owners.\n\nImage: Returning Uluru\n\nBase of Uluru climb in June 2014. Photo by author.\n\n# Returning Uluru\n\nDavid West\n\n### David West\n\nPublished Apr 21, 2021\n\n+ Follow\n\nOn 26 October 2019, the climb of Uluru was closed, following a decision in November 2017 by the Uluru-Kata Tjuta National Park Board of Management. This had been a long-standing objective of the traditional owners. Immediately following the closure, the climb infrastructure installed on Uluru in the 1960s and 1970s to make it 'safer' for tourists to climb Uluru was removed. This was done in order to return Uluru to a condition as close as possible to that which had prevailed for thousands of years.\n\nI’m privileged to have had the opportunity during 2019 to contribute to this process of removing the climb infrastructure, through a collaborative engagement of my business at the time, International Conservation Services, with Parks Australia and the contractor, Spire Solutions. My contribution was to review ways of removing as much as possible of the steel posts from the rock surface without causing further damage, and methods for protecting any remaining steel embedded in the rock from ongoing deterioration.\n\nThis work was described in a paper presented at the International Institute for Conservation (IIC) 2020 Edinburgh Congress by my colleague at International Conservation Services, Julian Bickersteth. The paper, co-authored by David Wallis of Spire Solutions and myself, is published here, and the online presentation given by Julian is available here.\n\nIt is an honour to reflect on my small contribution to the process of returning Uluru to the traditional owners.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVol. 41, No. 1, WINTER 2020, A QUIET DESPERATION: MODERN AGRICULTURE AND RURAL LIFE of Harv